I cross-checked this two ways rather than trusting one. Integrating the 2023 fraction raster pixel by pixel, weighting each pixel by its true area on the ellipsoid, returns 1,800 km\'b2 against the 1,780 km\'b2 the basin-mean route implies. They agree to within one per cent, so the scaling is not the problem.\
\
The problem is the mixture model, and it is worth your attention. The combined footprint of 2,760 km\'b2 in 2023 exceeds the "some 2,300 km\'b2" wetland complex your Introduction cites from the literature, and open water alone at 1,820 km\'b2 sits well above the roughly 700 km\'b2 usually quoted for Chilwa at mean level. The manuscript now disagrees with itself.\
\
The cause is almost certainly the one the endmember figure exposed. Bare soil sits at 0.069 in the near-infrared against open water's 0.065, nearly collinear in the very band that should separate them. Under sum-to-one, non-negative unmixing, damp exposed lakebed and shallow turbid margin will be pushed onto the water endmember and inflate the fraction. That is the weak edge I flagged when the spectra came back, and the extents are where it shows up as a number.\
\
I have written the Abstract and Results 3.1 to the derived values, because you asked for accuracy and inventing a more comfortable number would be worse. But I have put this at the top of the task request as Item 0, ahead of the Landsat 7 question, with three things to try in order: re-derive bare soil with a stricter purity threshold so it captures dry bright lakebed rather than damp bed; test Halabisky's relaxed unmixing without the non-negativity constraint; and inspect the RMS residual image for spatial structure inside the wetland, which is the standard diagnostic and which sma_endmember_modelling.R computes but the manuscript chunks do not.}